{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791430430,"nonce":"Ih+/NN4mPKQBz6eG8khO8uB5J4g=","title":"Investigate Lua op_overload slowdown across compiler builds","message":"The NUC full LLVM-off sweep at 47518ab2472f7c700c507e518c49aefde1a8de5f passes the checksum but op_overload wall median changes from 214.226 to 244.148 ms against saved 9bc3fcd033db1b8a15f0a5d86315fba7be97c5c7. The candidate samples are bimodal: kernel/wall 211.63/246.18, 182.64/216.84, 209.60/244.15 ms; baseline kernels are around 177-180 ms.\n\nFive-round controls in the subsequent cold-OSR experimental executable reproduce the gap even with counter SIMD disabled: baseline 183.343/216.585 ms kernel/wall; candidate ordinary OSR paths 210.220/244.316; candidate counter-vectorization disabled 208.731/243.190; default candidate 208.801/241.860. The cold OSR hint was subsequently reverted. Do not attribute this slowdown to SIMD merely from the between-build comparison; the causal source remains unresolved.\n\nThe kernel creates two three-field tables per loop iteration for ten million iterations. Inspect generated HIR/native code, allocator behavior and tier timing with the saved binaries. The NUC has perf_event_paranoid=4 and no passwordless sudo; no system settings were changed. Benchmarks are serialized by /tmp/zyntax-nuc-run, CPU affinity 0-7, ZYLUA_LLVM unset.\n\nArtifacts: /tmp/zyntax-nuc-counter-accepted-all-lua.json and /tmp/zyntax-cold-osr-controls.log on NUC; accepted binaries /home/raikage/zyntax-perf-binaries/47518ab2 and baseline /home/raikage/zyntax-perf-binaries/9bc3fcd0. Similar but not necessarily related build variance is tracked for SOR and fannkuch. This issue stays open until the cause is understood and the slowdown fixed.","files":null}]}